#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520536} :activity/id #uuid "2d4484de-f9fb-54c3-89bf-df23e280c65c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050166 :topic/id #uuid "3342477c-42d1-5dd5-8ebd-975b2a6a85a5" :topic/math-academy-id 1204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520536 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174b4041-f106-59ec-ab7a-44ef501bae0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520025} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7adb23-6c82-5afe-af17-e71cf492519e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ff347ada6a917e7d6da98f1f9ab39b484ac7e50211bbae597b21066a74b9282a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520054} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ee2a52-7af7-568a-838d-0419c87c99a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/77/77e6766deafc946e3b985fb3922a080a8ddad7f5be199fd314c6bf33d676149d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520214} {:answer/id #uuid "e833c9ed-ee14-5418-8a3c-defcce10db9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3c/3cb013ecb259c79858ae8705e06ddff54e5c0b5c01d11d5047fd62933e93baf2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520333} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b3ace1-61e2-532e-99a7-bee4fa63be58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1a099796621d60952dbd0e26a51222c8d9f29df5c3f8b5f141de759ff00bf825.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520025} :answer-field/id #uuid "82ad018d-6532-5573-be38-3543ae240b24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-20254" :question/problem "![](images/42/42e163dd119aaea37d2a373310b408a409023400a6f84ff520c464c6acb153b9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0.3)$ | $0.3$ | $(0.3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈-0.3.$ So ${f}^{″}(-0.3)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(-0.3)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈-0.3,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png)"} :step/id #uuid "2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520613}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a14fb76-81ef-5b51-a32c-50df3b3cebd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/84/84113b8f73965322a975402bbbd40f198036928e40f5865b00b01edb76ba898c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520131} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc14647-e2e3-5824-b3c6-c8f100a86208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520199} {:answer/id #uuid "a95ac06a-4df1-528f-96b0-e8e1a3d3a5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b6/b6a3eea0bb86d31c655f1108469d35b8091e6f7b2dafca8a5f3b73e316f0cc6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520224} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1513fe-facd-5469-826a-aceea9efe925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cf/cf426dd8f171cf12f1a7f6f9e15c622ebc84fa39511b8163474ece93de77a009.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520276} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ffc8cb-7b92-5d4c-abba-f07a6cdf7a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d1/d1d9c79b15d10a5035998809b714b480c87a0c13825aa8aadf0b8956ebbf90c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9efc5b3d-9312-5242-bda0-ac0535473480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" :question/math-academy-id "q-114644" :question/problem "![](images/53/53693867d697a4aaf7bedd2267ac2e77087b01a270f44ce68e9a4d303b81a5ad.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png)"} :step/id #uuid "4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520552}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822df945-069f-5212-b24c-a7224f617de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520181} {:answer/id #uuid "831caeb4-4e08-551b-af16-8bc85d4372ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/db/db832b68d0acb869dfb1431f3bf55acc1085b25babbdde5b9583eae1da9b49b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520183} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd164fc-a22a-56dc-ae5e-f763c3ff80fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/63/6368b0ec13a8859bcfcbdad59d6053605faffaf1b1d9b2069ad2ff6b22fbf778.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520258} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b0efff-abae-54cb-bfb3-65f66a460a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d8/d847b7acb517b10f70af82cad1054b586cacb245e2389ccfd3296c244dfab6cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520268} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e60351-e333-5a6b-be45-1d4de70bae1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/13fd6b12240b83c9cffb7fbdaa5d75750b1686ec97a6b4085a6f05dc72c010cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520181} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5af535f-d80a-5c38-9d0e-a84eb6995c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" :question/math-academy-id "q-114759" :question/problem "![](images/67/67cfcaae560edcf2fda8c69770088113b6035b46c9951f67ef81f13b8ea9615e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are positive and negative, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07db90e3-0a59-5db0-b415-ff2cc2acfbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/21/2120febb62cb220085338423b3677c965ef05de4a03c8e15a003317e787af1e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520003} {:answer/id #uuid "2456fff0-2a4b-5357-8c10-186ee0d990c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520044} {:answer/id #uuid "33647010-0ca9-5c0e-86d3-15e55a578800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d9/d9a9d1fce2a8e02903fcdd1dc0d83c49fa7ab9c8fbfb3aaf7ccdc6f292accb14.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520067} {:answer/id #uuid "44735508-9483-5b63-a255-6a13f9fbf3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e475a76051bdb6f7e30c6b34319d7409afd5cf6af3ffdcfd1d5d07d93d31f58c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520090} {:answer/id #uuid "c3305299-f594-5d0f-9ba5-a922f8fdab0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/47/47c0127e6829858e84bbf4e89c22b7248ca95f16a6fd967831aedba4b5c142f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520044} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0b2e54-91a9-512b-93ce-8c8288271db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-114627" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe369706a9e211bfc5265041bbc5d2ec0fab69eb205c23111f548785a2f88708.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ So ${f}^{″}(0)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(0)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈0,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png)"} :step/id #uuid "54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea3aa539-ef2e-5c7d-8a06-629c168c575e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/86/867584e020e3d8a3b3441f40310adb06a7889363235e8153f3f18c38df66b5f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100060} {:answer/id #uuid "021331f4-2b20-5ac9-b8ba-872f33b9c32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6d/6d1c580bdac480eca405cebcc9bce8c3e40a0990ae1ac92b8f17d067c9e5df1b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100061} {:answer/id #uuid "1a45ec37-a938-50af-b59e-466e06d5231f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/87/877fcf017ab91010648de9a375e535ec7697841c8e317196c5759df6aca32e19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100062} {:answer/id #uuid "696b675a-cc16-58f3-9dec-72e8d5dcaf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8c/8c3f90a7167dee9905e6f0fc7861dd9dc06b1f731064102e31a386975b11a6c8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100063} {:answer/id #uuid "732af8ef-5771-5509-b4ce-1c057103b77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0e/0e3de4e98c4c04b37ea5e3201e5ea3d131fbc3ca126f90c4e2deccc40c41bd8a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100062} :answer-field/id #uuid "e408fd18-0582-5135-ac47-487dc0d8ab14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "986f6f9d-dbf1-5fb1-85d5-0cfcc16f723b" :question/math-academy-id "q-51415" :question/problem "![](images/17/174178e1d4c527bb81ea3ba726e5ef753b40dd85b452d65faf05590e7a387a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to $0$) when we pass through $x=4$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=4$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/877fcf017ab91010648de9a375e535ec7697841c8e317196c5759df6aca32e19.png)"} :step/id #uuid "e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520553}}] :activity/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519987}]}
